hat developed in South Asia under Muslim rulers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indo-pakistani-war-of-1965"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indo-pakistani-war-of-1965.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indo-Pakistani War of 1965</span><span class="enc-type">War · 1965</span><span class="enc-snip">The Indo-Pakistani War of 1965 was fought between India and Pakistan from August to September 1965.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/indo-persian-culture"><span class="enc-name">Indo-Persian culture</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Indo-Persian culture refers to the synthesis of Persian and Indian traditions that flourished in South Asia, especially at Muslim courts.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indo-scythians"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indo-scythians.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indo-Scythians</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Indo-Scythians were Saka groups who moved from Central Asia through Sistan and Gandhara into north-western India from the late second century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indochina"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indochina.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indochina</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Indochina, or French Indochina, was the federation of French colonial territories in South-East Asia comprising Vietnam's three regions, Cambodia and Laos.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indochina-university"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indochina-university.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indochina University</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">Indochina University was the first modern university in French Indochina, founded in Hanoi in 1906 under Governor-General Paul Beau.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indochinese-communist-party"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indochinese-communist-party.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indochinese Communist Party</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Indochinese Communist Party was the communist party founded by Ho Chi Minh in Hong Kong in 1930, merging rival Vietnamese communist groups.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indonesia"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indonesia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indonesia</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Indonesia is an archipelago nation in South-East Asia comprising thousands of islands, including Java and Sumatra.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/indonesia-raya"><span class="enc-name">Indonesia Raya</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">&quot;Indonesia Raya&quot; (&quot;Great Indonesia&quot;) was written by Wage Rudolf Supratman, a musician and young reporter.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-independence"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indonesian-independence.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indonesian independence</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">Indonesian independence was proclaimed in 1945 and recognised by the Netherlands in 1949 after a period of armed conflict and negotiation.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-armed-forces"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indonesian-national-armed-forces.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indonesian National Armed Forces</span><span class="enc-type">Institution</span><span class="enc-snip">The Indonesian National Armed Forces (Tentara Nasional Indonesia, TNI) are the army, navy and air force of Indonesia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-party"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indonesian-national-party.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indonesian National Party</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Indonesian National Party (PNI) was founded by Sukarno and others in Bandung on 4 July 1927 to win independence through mass non-cooperation with the Dutch.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indonesian-national-revolution"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indonesian-national-revolution.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indonesian National Revolution</span><span class="enc-type">Revolution · War · 1947–1949</span><span class="enc-snip">The Indonesian National Revolution was the armed and diplomatic struggle for independence from Dutch rule, fought from 1947 to 1949 in its main phase.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indra"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indra</span><span class="enc-type">Religion / idea</span><span class="enc-snip">Indra is the king of the gods in the Rigveda, the god of thunder and rain who wields the thunderbolt and slays the serpent Vritra to release the waters.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/indra-iii"><span class="enc-name">Indra III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Indra III was the Rashtrakuta emperor of the Deccan from 914 to 929.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/indra-iv"><span class="enc-name">Indra IV</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Indra IV was the last claimant of the Rashtrakuta dynasty, a grandson of Krishna III.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indra-jatra"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indra-jatra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indra Jatra</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">Indra Jatra is an annual festival in Kathmandu, Nepal, held in late summer or early autumn in honour of the god Indra.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indragiri-hilir-regency"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indragiri-hilir-regency.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indragiri Hilir Regency</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Indragiri Hilir is a regency on the east coast of Sumatra in Riau province, Indonesia, covering the lower course and delta of the Indragiri River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indragiri-hulu-regency"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indragiri-hulu-regency.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indragiri Hulu Regency</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Indragiri Hulu is a regency in Riau province on Sumatra, Indonesia, covering the upper course of the Indragiri River.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indragiri-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indragiri-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indragiri River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Indragiri River rises in the Minangkabau highlands of West Sumatra, where it is called the Kuantan, and flows east through Riau province into the Strait of Malacca.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indrapura-champa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indrapura-champa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indrapura (Champa)</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Indrapura was a capital of Champa in central Vietnam, at Đồng Dương in Quảng Nam province, from the late 9th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indravarma"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indravarma.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indravarma</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Indravarma was a ruler of the Apracharaja dynasty of Bajaur in the north-west of the Indian subcontinent around the turn of the era.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/indravarman-ii"><span class="enc-name">Indravarman II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Indravarman II was a ruler of the Khmer Empire and the son of Jayavarman VII.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/indravarman-iii"><span class="enc-name">Indravarman III</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Indravarman III, also titled Srindravarman, ruled the Khmer Empire from 1295 to 1308.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/indrayudha"><span class="enc-name">Indrayudha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Indrayudha was a king of Kannauj in northern India in the late 8th century, the first known ruler of the Ayudha dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/induna"><span class="enc-name">Induna</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Induna, plural iziNduna, is a Zulu and Xhosa title meaning advisor, headman, ambassador or commander of a group of warriors.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indus"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indus</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Indus is a great river of South Asia flowing from Tibet through Pakistan to the Arabian Sea.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indus-script"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indus-script.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indus script</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Indus script, also called the Harappan script, is the set of signs used by the Indus Valley Civilisation, found chiefly on stamp seals, seal impressions and pottery.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley"><span class="enc-name">Indus Valley</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Indus Valley is the region along the Indus River in present-day Pakistan and north-western India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/indus-valley-civilisation.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Indus Valley Civilisation</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Indus Valley Civilisation was a Bronze Age civilisation in the Indus basin, known for planned cities such as Harappa and Mohenjo-daro.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/industrial-and-commercial-workers-union"><span class="enc-name">Industrial and Commercial Workers' Union</span><span class="enc-type">People / group</span><span class="enc-snip">The Industrial and Commercial Workers' Union was a trade union founded by Clements Kadalie in Cape Town in 1919, beginning among dock workers.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/industrial-revolution.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Industrial Revolution</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Industrial Revolution was the transition to mechanised production, steam power and factory industry that began in Britain in the eighteenth century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/indutiomarus"><span class="enc-name">Indutiomarus</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Indutiomarus was a leader of the Treveri, a Gallic people of the Moselle valley, during Julius Caesar's Gallic Wars.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/ine-of-wessex"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ine-of-wessex.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Ine of Wessex</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Ine of Wessex was king of the West Saxons from 688 to 726, one of the strongest Anglo-Saxon rulers before Alfred.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/infamous-decade"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/infamous-decade.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Infamous Decade</span><span class="enc-type">Event</span><span class="enc-snip">The Infamous Decade is the name given to the period of Argentine history from the coup of 1930 to the military revolution of 1943.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/infaq-al-maysur"><span class="enc-name">Infaq al-Maysur</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Infaq al-Maysur is a historical work by Muhammad Bello, the second ruler o